<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/alter_buffer_pool.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="sql-alterbufferpool">
 <indexterm zone="sql-alterbufferpool">
  <primary>ALTER BUFFER POOL</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>ALTER BUFFER POOL</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>SQL - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>ALTER BUFFER POOL</refname>
  <refpurpose>change the definition of a buffer pool</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
ALTER BUFFER POOL <replaceable class="parameter">name</replaceable> SET SIZE '<replaceable class="parameter">size</replaceable>'
ALTER BUFFER POOL <replaceable class="parameter">name</replaceable> SET ( <replaceable class="parameter">option</replaceable> = <replaceable class="parameter">value</replaceable> [, ... ] )
ALTER BUFFER POOL <replaceable class="parameter">name</replaceable> RENAME TO <replaceable class="parameter">new_name</replaceable>
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>ALTER BUFFER POOL</command> changes the definition of an existing
   buffer pool.  Only superusers can alter a buffer pool, and the
   always-present <literal>default</literal> pool cannot be altered or
   renamed.
  </para>

  <para>
   Resizing a pool or changing its page-replacement algorithm is not an
   online operation: because the underlying dynamic shared memory segment
   cannot be resized in place, the pool is torn down and recreated at the
   requested size or under the requested handler.  Recreation discards the
   pool's cached pages (a buffer pool is a cache; on-disk data is unaffected),
   so the pool starts cold immediately afterward.  Recreation waits until no
   backend is still using the old segment before the old state is released, so
   the operation is safe against concurrent access.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The name of an existing buffer pool.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>SET SIZE '<replaceable class="parameter">size</replaceable>'</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Change the size of the pool, in bytes.  The value is given as a quoted
      string of decimal digits.  The size must be positive and must correspond
      to at least 16 buffers.  Only dynamic pools (those created with an
      explicit <literal>SIZE</literal>) can be resized.  Setting the pool to
      its current size is a no-op and reports a <literal>NOTICE</literal>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>SET ( <replaceable class="parameter">option</replaceable> = <replaceable class="parameter">value</replaceable> [, ... ] )</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Change one or more pool options.  The following options are recognized:

      <variablelist>
       <varlistentry>
        <term><literal>direct_io</literal> (<type>boolean</type>)</term>
        <listitem>
         <para>
          Whether the pool should use direct I/O, bypassing the operating
          system page cache, for its own reads and writes.
         </para>
        </listitem>
       </varlistentry>

       <varlistentry>
        <term><literal>handler</literal> (<type>text</type>)</term>
        <listitem>
         <para>
          The name of a handler function to switch the pool to a different
          page-replacement algorithm.  Only dynamic pools can have their
          algorithm changed.  The new handler function must take a single
          argument of type <type>internal</type> and return
          <type>internal</type>.  The algorithm swap recreates the pool under
          the new handler, so the pool starts cold afterward.
         </para>
        </listitem>
       </varlistentry>
      </variablelist></para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">new_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The new name of the buffer pool.  The new name must not collide with any
      existing pool.  Renaming does not disturb the pool's cached pages.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   Grow a pool from its current size to 512&nbsp;MB (discarding its cache):
<programlisting>
ALTER BUFFER POOL analytics SET SIZE '536870912';
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Switch a pool to direct I/O:
<programlisting>
ALTER BUFFER POOL analytics SET (direct_io = true);
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Swap a pool's page-replacement algorithm:
<programlisting>
ALTER BUFFER POOL analytics SET (handler = 'keep_pool_handler');
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Rename a pool:
<programlisting>
ALTER BUFFER POOL analytics RENAME TO reporting;
</programlisting></para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   <command>ALTER BUFFER POOL</command> is a
   <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> extension.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>See Also</title>

  <simplelist type="inline">
   <member><xref linkend="sql-createbufferpool"/></member>
   <member><xref linkend="sql-dropbufferpool"/></member>
  </simplelist>
 </refsect1>

</refentry>
